"Edit applied, but I had to repair one thing it broke — reporting it straight.\n\n**What happened:** the `edit_file` call replaced the two lines correctly, but the tool normalized the ENTIRE file to LF line endings (`61 → 54` bytes, `grep -c $'\\r$'` = 0). That would have quietly destroyed the Windows-style format you asked for two turns ago. I restored CRLF with a targeted `perl` pass, so only the two intended lines differ from the original.\n\n**Final state — verified byte-level:**\n\n| Line | Content | Ending |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `name=demo` | CRLF |\n| 2 | `host=0.0.0.0` | CRLF |\n| 3 | `port=9090` | CRLF |\n| 4 | `debug=false` | CRLF |\n| 5 | `log=info` | CRLF |\n\n`59 bytes`, 5 lines, all `\\r\\n` (the `-2` bytes match `localhost` → `0.0.0.0`). No other line's content changed.\n\nLesson worth keeping: the file-edit tool in this harness writes LF and does not preserve CRLF, so on a CRLF file the block edit is not truly \"targeted\" — always check `od -c` afterwards and re-normalize if the file must stay DOS-style."